::ALG2_U01_L03_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L03_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5<br><br>Question:<br>Factor 6x<sup>3</sup> + 9x<sup>2</sup>. {
=3x<sup>2</sup>(2x + 3) #Correct. The greatest common factor is 3x squared.
~3x(2x<sup>2</sup> + 3x) #This factors out 3x, but it does not use the greatest common factor.
~x<sup>2</sup>(6x + 9) #This misses the numerical common factor 3.
~3x<sup>2</sup>(2x - 3) #The sign inside the factor should be positive because both original terms are positive.
}

::ALG2_U01_L03_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L03_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5<br><br>Question:<br>Factor x<sup>2</sup> - 49. {
~(x - 7)(x - 7) #This expands to x squared - 14x + 49, not x squared - 49.
~(x + 7)(x + 7) #This expands to x squared + 14x + 49.
=(x - 7)(x + 7) #Correct. This is the difference of squares x squared minus 7 squared.
~x(x - 49) #This expands to x squared - 49x, not x squared - 49.
}

::ALG2_U01_L03_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L03_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5<br><br>Question:<br>Factor x<sup>2</sup> + 7x + 12. {
~(x + 2)(x + 6) #This multiplies to 12 but the middle term would be 8x.
=(x + 3)(x + 4) #Correct. 3 and 4 multiply to 12 and add to 7.
~(x - 3)(x - 4) #This gives a positive constant but a negative middle term.
~(x + 1)(x + 12) #This multiplies to 12 but the middle term would be 13x.
}

::ALG2_U01_L03_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L03_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5<br><br>Question:<br>Factor x<sup>2</sup> - x - 20. {
~(x - 4)(x + 5) #This gives a middle term of x, not -x.
~(x - 10)(x + 2) #This multiplies to -20 but gives a middle term of -8x.
~(x - 4)(x - 5) #This gives a positive constant, not -20.
=(x + 4)(x - 5) #Correct. 4 and -5 multiply to -20 and add to -1.
}

::ALG2_U01_L03_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L03_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.1; MLA.A2.AE.5<br><br>Question:<br>Which factored form is equivalent to x<sup>2</sup> + 8x + 12? {
~(x + 1)(x + 12) #This expands to x squared + 13x + 12.
=(x + 2)(x + 6) #Correct. 2 and 6 multiply to 12 and add to 8.
~(x - 2)(x - 6) #This expands to x squared - 8x + 12.
~(x + 3)(x + 4) #This expands to x squared + 7x + 12.
}
